window.docContents[110].resolve({"/Design-restrictions/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions":{"contents":"Core C++ is a subset of C++17, and every Core C++ program compiles with g++ -std=c++17. Each restriction of the subset removes a construct, or a freedom that C++17 leaves to the compiler. This chapter states, for each restriction, what C++17 allows, what Core C++ admits, and the property of the semantics the restriction gives.\n\nThe properties are those the earlier chapters state. Every construct has a typing rule and an evaluation rule. A program has at most one result, . A well typed program has a derivation, does not terminate, or reaches a case that the evaluator reports as error, . Neither statement has a Lean proof yet, so both are properties checked by inspection of the rule set.\n\nThe behaviour of C++ in this chapter comes from N4659, the final working draft of C++17, open-std.org. It also comes from g++ -std=c++17, which on the machine of these tests is Apple clang 21.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Core C++","header":"9. Design restrictions","id":"/Design-restrictions/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions"}});